// T034 / CT008 — a `<<` / `>>` whose shift AMOUNT is `@SecretCT` is rejected.
// expect-error: T034
//
// A shift by a data-dependent count is variable-time on cores without a
// barrel shifter and on microcoded shift paths, so the count leaks through
// timing. That is the class the `@SecretCT` sublattice promises to reject
// ("variable-time operations on secret-tainted operands"), and until this
// rule landed nothing did: this program compiled with an empty diagnostic set
// on main `ae026aec` (measured 2026-09-20; closed 2026-09-30).
//
// Only the AMOUNT (the right operand) is checked. Shifting a `@SecretCT` VALUE
// by a `@Public` amount stays legal: that latency depends on the public count
// alone, never on the bits being moved, and it is exactly how constant-time
// code masks and rotates a secret. Banning it would ban the sound idiom.
//
// The check is on the operand's taint LABEL, not its syntactic form, so a
// `let`-copied or arithmetic-derived `@SecretCT` amount is rejected the same
// way, and the compound forms `<<=` / `>>=` desugar to the same node.
//
// The MUTATION_SITE below is the parameter list. Swap the `@SecretCT`
// annotation from `n` to `a` (`a: i64 @SecretCT, n: i64`) and the fixture
// compiles cleanly, proving this pins the secret AMOUNT specifically and not
// merely the presence of `@SecretCT` on a shift operand.
module main;

// MUTATION_SITE
fn f(a: i64, n: i64 @SecretCT) -> i64 @SecretCT {
    return a << n;
}
